@node MainReader "The Reader"
@prev Contents
@next Project

AmigaGuide on Linux is a modern reader for AmigaGuide documents.

The reader is intended to remain focused on reading AmigaGuide documents while supporting modern platforms. Current work targets Linux and Windows, with future investigation of AROS and AmigaOS support.

The reader should also be able to resolve optional library references without becoming dependent on the library infrastructure.
@endnode

@node Project "The Project"
@prev MainReader
@next Ecosystem

The project is an open-source effort to give AmigaGuide a useful modern life while preserving the character of the original format.

The long-term goal is not simply to reproduce a legacy viewer. It is to make AmigaGuide useful again as a portable, interconnected hypertext and documentation format.

A separate AmigaGuide Editor is planned as a Version 2 goal. The editor should be able to create new guides and edit existing guides while understanding AmigaGuide structure rather than acting only as a generic text editor.
@endnode

@node Ecosystem "The AmigaGuide Ecosystem"
@prev Project
@next Library

The reader, editor, library, crawler, and conversion tools are intended to remain separate projects connected by the AmigaGuide format and a small common reference mechanism.

A possible ecosystem is:

Reader
  |
  +-- local AmigaGuide documents
  +-- ordinary web links
  +-- library references

Library
  |
  +-- curated AmigaGuide documents
  +-- metadata and provenance
  +-- stable identifiers

Tools
  |
  +-- PDF to AmigaGuide
  +-- HTML/text to AmigaGuide
  +-- OCR for scanned documents
  +-- validation and indexing
  +-- crawler/catalog tools

The collection could contain classic Amiga material, older public-domain works, government documents, historical texts, technical references, modern material where licensing permits, and original AmigaGuide content.
@endnode

@node Library "The AmigaGuide Library"
@prev Ecosystem
@next References

The proposed AmigaGuide Library is a searchable collection of guides rather than part of the reader itself.

Each document should have stable metadata including title, author, date, source, license or copyright status, provenance, topics, and a stable identifier.

A conceptual library reference is:

  ag://example.document.identifier

The physical copy could live locally, on a LAN, or on an Internet server without changing the identifier.

This would allow citations between AmigaGuide documents. For example, a guide discussing United States history could link to an AmigaGuide edition of the Constitution, Declaration of Independence, a Supreme Court opinion, or another primary source when an appropriate guide exists.

The library and crawler must treat licensing and provenance as first-class metadata. Publicly accessible material is not automatically public domain.
@endnode

@node References "Related Ideas"
@prev Library
@next Contents

The project roadmap includes:

- Reader navigation and history improvements
- Search and indexing
- A standalone AmigaGuide Editor
- A curated AmigaGuide Library
- Stable GUID-based library references
- A separate crawler and indexer
- PDF, HTML, and text conversion tools
- OCR support for scanned documents
- Cross-document citations and references
- AROS and AmigaOS 3.x/4.x integration
- Safe handling of external AmigaGuide commands

The security model is important because legacy AmigaGuide documents can contain commands capable of invoking external functionality. Untrusted documents should not silently execute arbitrary system or ARexx commands.

This guide is intentionally self-referential: it documents the AmigaGuide on Linux project using AmigaGuide itself.
@endnode

